      "quiz_silhouette_img": "Silhouette of the bird you are being asked to identify",
      "sa_alt": "Timeline from March to May. Each species is a bar spanning its arrival window, sorted earliest first. Blackbirds, robins and grackles arrive in March; loons and the first flycatchers in April; most warblers, thrushes and flycatchers cluster in mid May.",
      "sa_apr": "APR",
      "sa_mar": "MAR",
      "sa_may": "MAY",
      "sa_note": "Sorted by arrival, not alphabetically, so the wave is visible: a thin early trickle of blackbirds and robins, then a broad pile-up in mid May when most insect-eaters arrive at once. Bars show the window each species is expected in, not a single date — arrival shifts with the season and with the weather.",
      "sa_title": "The spring wave, in order of arrival",
      "sb_bill": "Bill:",
      "sb_down": "down-curved",
      "sb_headwide": "head width",
      "sb_legs": "Legs:",
      "sb_long": "long",
      "sb_med": "medium",
      "sb_note": "Every shorebird here is drawn on the same body at the same scale, so bill length, bill curve and leg length can be compared directly. Those three plus leg colour settle most of this family; plumage changes with the season, but the proportions do not.",
      "sb_short": "short",
      "sb_straight": "straight",
      "sb_title": "Drawn to one scale — compare, do not memorise",
      "sb_up": "slightly upturned",
      "shape_img": "Bird body-shape sketch",
      "sketch_d1": "Bill",
      "sketch_d2": "Eye",
      "sketch_d3": "Wing pattern",
      "sketch_d4": "Tail shape",
      "sketch_d5": "Feet",
      "sketch_detail_alt": "A pencil sketch of a perched bird with five numbered callouts: bill, eye, wing pattern, tail shape, feet.",
      "sketch_note1": "Head about half the body. Set the gap between them before anything else.",
      "sketch_note2": "Connect the ovals. The dashed line is the body angle — that angle is a field mark.",
      "sketch_note3": "Now the details, in order. Stop here and you already have a usable sketch.",
      "sketch_note4": "Wing line, then plumage marks last. About 1-2 minutes total.",
      "sketch_step1": "Two ovals",
      "sketch_step2": "Join + posture line",
      "sketch_step3": "Bill, eye, tail, legs",
      "sketch_step4": "Wing + marks",
      "sp_head_note": "Sparrows are told apart by the face. Compare the crown, the eyebrow, the line through the eye and the throat against the next bird before looking at anything else.",
      "sp_head_title": "Head pattern",
      "spread_dist": "Distraction display",
      "spread_dist_note": "ONE wing splayed and dragging, the bird stumbling AWAY from the nest. The other wing stays folded.",
      "spread_mantle": "Mantling",
      "spread_mantle_note": "Wings arched FORWARD and down like a tent, head low. There is prey underneath, being hidden from thieves.",
      "spread_sun": "Sun-bathing",
      "spread_sun_note": "Wings open FLAT and turned to the sun, tail fanned, feathers raised. Nothing underneath the bird.",
      "spread_wing_intro": "Sun-bathing and mantling are both defined below as a \"spread-wing posture\", which is not enough to separate them in the field. What actually differs is where the wings go and what is underneath the bird.",
      "spread_wing_title": "Three \"wings out\" postures — how to tell them apart",
      "wax_bohemian": "Bohemian Waxwing",
      "wax_bohemian_note": "Rusty undertail. Greyer body, white + yellow wing marks.",
      "wax_cedar": "Cedar Waxwing",
      "wax_cedar_note": "White undertail. Warmer brown, yellow belly, plain wing.",
      "wax_key": "Check the undertail first — rusty means Bohemian, white means Cedar. It is the one mark that holds up at distance and in poor winter light.",
      "wax_tag_rusty": "rusty undertail",
      "wax_tag_white": "white undertail",
      "wb_plate_note": "Every warbler here is drawn on the same body, so what differs on screen is what differs in the field: throat, face, wing bars, sides and rump. Females and autumn birds are duller — the shape and the wing bars survive, the colours often do not.",
      "wb_plate_title": "Breeding male",
      "wf_female": "♀ Female",
      "wf_male": "♂ Male",
      "wf_note": "Both sexes are drawn because the female is the harder call — most are brown, and they are separated on head shape, bill shape and where the pale patches sit rather than on colour. Look at the outline before the plumage.",
      "wing3d_aspect": "Aspect ratio: ",
      "wing3d_aspect_note": "Span divided by average width. High means long and thin — cheap to fly far, useless in dense cover. Low means short and broad — costly over distance, but it turns.",
      "wing3d_failed": "3D view unavailable on this device or network. Every wing is a button above and every fact about it is in the cards below — nothing here needs the picture.",
      "wing3d_label": "Wing shape, 3D. Interactive. Arrow keys rotate, plus and minus zoom, zero resets. Every wing here is also a button, and every fact about it is in the card below.",
      "wing3d_loading": "Loading the wing model…",
      "wing3d_look": "Look for: ",
      "wing3d_look_droop": "Front on: the wings are not a flat plank. They fall away from the shoulder, and how much they do that differs by wing.",
      "wing3d_look_slots_no": "This wing has no slotted fingers — the outer edge stays one clean surface. Switch to the soaring wing to compare.",
      "wing3d_look_slots_yes": "Look from below: the outer feathers separate into upswept fingers. Those slots are what let a soaring bird stay up at low speed.",
      "wing3d_look_span": "Look along the span from the wingtip to see how much of the wing is length and how much is width.",
      "wing3d_preset_above": "⬒ From above — see the planform",
      "wing3d_preset_front": "◡ Front on — see the droop",
      "wing3d_title": "Turn the wing over — planform, sweep and slots in 3D",
      "wp_plate_note": "Woodpeckers sort first on what the back is doing — a white ladder stripe, zebra bars, solid black, or brown spots — and only then on which patch of the head is red. Bills are drawn to scale against the head.",
      "wp_plate_title": "Back pattern + head marks",
